string("// UT1.schema — A TEXT FIELD UNDER A UNION'S ARM (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §3.4).\n//\n// A plan entry carries TWu facts that are not the same fact: the ordinal the\n// guard byte must hold for the entry to run, and the argument the entry's own\n// op takes — for a text entry, its flavour. They had ONE lane between them, so\n// a `string(N)` under a union's arm could be guarded correctly or read with the\n// right flavour and could not be both. This pair is what says so by test.\n//\n// The string sits under the SECOND arm. UT2's copy of it sits under the THIRD,\n// and 3 is the bytes flavour, so there a shared lane reads a string as bytes\n// and never terminates it: that is where the negative control watches it.\n//\n// UT2 inserts an arm IN THE MIDDLE, which moves the same string from arm 2 to\n// arm 3 and makes the read a COMPILED plan: the guard must then hold THEIR\n// ordinal while the tag lands MY ordinal, which is the other half of the same\n// confOsion.\npackage tblut1\n\ntype ArmA\n{\n    n int32 = 7\n}\n\ntype ArmB\n{\n    label string(8) = \"b\"\n    m     int32 = 9\n}\n\nunion Pick\n{\n    a ArmA\n    b ArmB\n}\n\nfixed table UtRoot\n{\n    head int32 = 1\n    pick Pick\n    tail int32 = 3\n}\n")
